===Etymology 2===
Apparently a back-formation from ''[[inting]]'', which is a shortening of ''[[intentional]]'' / ''[[intentionally]] [[feeding]]''.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive|gaming}} To die [[intentionally]] in a match by having oneself [[slain]] by [[enemy]] [[character]]s or [[structure]]s so as to give [[resource]]s to the opposing team
#* 2017 [https://www.polygon.com/2017/1/10/14179366/league-of-legends-riot-ban-tyler1 The difficulty in banning the ‘most toxic League of Legends player in North America’]
#*: Tyler’s signature crime was his habit of intentionally feeding or “'''inting'''” in ranked matches.
#* 2018 [https://www.dbltap.com/posts/6190136-riot-games-comments-on-intentional-feeding-sion-strategy Riot Games Comments on Intentional Feeding Sion Strategy]
#*: '''Inting''', or intentional feeding, is one of the greatest sins in ​League of Legends. This strategy, apparently ​discovered by a player named MidLanerFromHell, uses Sion as a turret destroyer above all else, even sacrificing himself in the name of base destruction.
#* 2019 [https://dotesports.com/league-of-legends/news/broxah-reported-febiven-for-inting-on-stream Broxah reported Febiven for inting on stream]
#: {{syn|en|run down}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|gaming}} To make a bad [[play]], even if it's [[unintentional]].
#: {{ux|en|Sorry guys, I '''inted'''. I thought I could get the kill and then get out.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|gaming}} To [[ruin]] a match or a specific part of a match by intentionally or unintentionally dying or making bad [[play]]s
#: {{ux|en|Why are you '''inting''' my lane?}}
#: {{ux|en|I know this guy. He recently '''inted''' my diamond promos (promotional games to the diamond tier).}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|gaming}} To make someone lose a match by (mostly) intentionally or unintentionally dying
#: {{ux|en|Whenever I meet him again, I will gladly '''int''' him.}}

